Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 2, verse 286:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Our Lord, do not hold us accountable if we forget or make mistakes﴾ [al-Baqarah: 286]. He did not clarify here whether He answered this supplication of theirs or not. But He indicated that He answered it by His saying regarding mistakes: ﴿And there is no blame upon you for what you have done by mistake﴾ [al-Ahzab: 5], up to the end of the verse. And He indicated that He answered it regarding forgetfulness by His saying: ﴿And if Satan causes you to forget, then do not sit after the reminder with the wrongdoing people﴾ [al-An'am: 68]. This clearly indicates that before the reminder there is no sin upon him in that. Nor does it weaken this that the verse ﴿And if Satan causes you to forget﴾ is Meccan, while the verse ﴿Do not hold us accountable if we forget﴾ is Medinan, since there is no barrier to clarifying the Medinan by the Meccan, or the reverse.

It is established in Sahih Muslim that when the Prophet (ﷺ) recited ﴿Our Lord, do not hold us accountable if we forget or make mistakes﴾, Allah, exalted is He, said: "Yes" (na'am).

* *

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Our Lord, and do not lay upon us a burden as You laid upon those before us﴾. He did not clarify here whether He answered this supplication of theirs or not, and He did not specify the burden (isr) that was laid upon those before us. But He made clear that He answered this supplication of theirs in other places, such as:

• His saying: ﴿And He relieves them of their burden and the shackles that were upon them﴾ [al-A'raf: 157];

• His saying: ﴿Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity﴾;

• His saying: ﴿And He has not placed upon you any difficulty in the religion﴾ [al-Hajj: 78];

• His saying: ﴿Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-Baqarah: 185];

and other verses besides these.

He indicated some of the burden (isr) that was laid upon those before us by His saying: ﴿So repent to your Creator and kill yourselves﴾ [al-Baqarah: 54], because making the killing of oneself a condition for the acceptance of repentance is among the greatest of burdens. Al-isr means the weight in obligation, and from it is the saying of al-Nabigha:

[Basit]

O preventer of oppression from reaching their nobles, and the one who bears the burden from them after they had known it.
